<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Stand on the Ugra River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1480">1480</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Stand on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a> was a stand-off in 1480 between the forces of Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and the army of the Great Horde on opposite banks of the Ugra River. The Horde's army withdrew without a decisive battle. The episode is traditionally taken to mark the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> overlordship over Muscovy, a milestone in the foundation story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-stand-on-the-ugra-river.jpg" alt="Great Stand on the Ugra River" width="227" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.16,_p._462_-_Great_standing_on_the_Ugra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The main Russian defence line ran along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka River</a> from Kaluga east towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>, and was extended westward along the Ugra. The land west and south of Kaluga was claimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Ivan III was uniting the lands north of the Oka while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> broke up and its steppe remnant came to be called the Great Horde.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> was allied with the Great Horde, and Muscovy with the Crimean Khanate. Akhmat raided the Oka at Aleksin in 1472 and was driven off, and in 1476 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> officially stopped paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. A quarrel in late 1479 between Ivan and his brothers Andrey Bolshoy and Boris of Volotsk may have influenced Akhmat's decision to attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>News of the invasion reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in late May 1480. One faction wanted to flee north but was overruled. Ivan sent troops to the Oka, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a>, Tarusa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a>, and later moved them west towards Kaluga. Around 30 September he returned to Moscow to consult his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and boyars, settled the quarrel with his brothers, and moved the treasury and royal family north to Beloozero.</p><p>Akhmat camped at Vorotynsk and waited for Casimir, who never came because he was tied down fighting the Crimeans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. On 6 to 8 October Akhmat moved up to the Ugra, and fighting lasted almost four days. His attempts to cross failed, largely because of Russian missiles, and the river was wide enough to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> arrows ineffective. Talks led nowhere but gave Ivan time to bring up more troops.</p><p>On 26 October Ivan began to pull back from the Ugra to Kremenets and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borovsk">Borovsk</a>, where he had a good position to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. On 8 November Akhmat began to withdraw, and news reached Ivan on 11 November. Akhmat raided twelve Lithuanian towns on the way. Ivan returned to Moscow on 28 November. Reasons for Akhmat's withdrawal are guesswork: Casimir's absence, the end of Ivan's quarrel with his brothers, the coming winter, disease, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> preference for raids over attacking a concentrated army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Akhmat was killed on 6 January 1481 in a clash with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibak-khan">Ibak Khan</a>. In 1502 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> destroyed the Great Horde as an organisation, which led to Russo-Crimean wars that lasted until 1784. Russian historiography treats the stand as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> yoke. Some modern historians regard it as indecisive: Charles Halperin noted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> had probably ceased paying tribute to the Great Horde in the 1470s, and Janet Martin called its significance embellished. After it, no Russian prince needed to ask a khan's permission to rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Stand on the Ugra River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moscow under Ivan III stopped paying tribute, and the <b>Great Stand on the Ugra River</b> in 1480 ended without battle when Khan Ahmed withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His forces crushed Novgorod at the Battle of the Shelon River in 1471, and in 1480 he faced Akhmat Khan of the Great Horde across the Ugra River until the Tatars withdrew without a battle, an episode remembered as the <b>Great Stand on the Ugra River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Golden Horde fractured into rival khanates and lost its hold over Muscovy, an end marked symbolically by the <b>Great Stand on the Ugra River</b> in 1480.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1480 Ahmad faced Ivan III's army on the <b>Ugra River</b> and withdrew without battle, ending Muscovy's tribute.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-horde">Great Horde</a></li><li><blockquote>Its most notable ruler, Ibak Khan, took part in the destruction of the Great Horde, killing its khan Ahmad in 1481 after the <b>Great Stand on the Ugra River</b>, and claimed leadership among the Jochids.</blockquote><a href="/state/tyumen-khanate">Tyumen Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
